1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,060
We climb the gallows steps as the blade falls and have a seat in the electric chair.

2
00:00:08,061 --> 00:00:12,040
The gas rises and the rifles roar.

3
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:19,120
The chemicals flow and we descend into a macabre world of dark and deadly inventions.

4
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:23,060
Now, Death Devices on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,680
Huntsville, Texas

6
00:00:37,681 --> 00:00:41,960
Lethal injection capital of the world

7
00:00:41,961 --> 00:00:46,900
Where more prisoners are legally put to death

8
00:00:46,901 --> 00:00:49,000
Than in any other American prison

9
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:53,000
During the 1980s and 90s

10
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:56,000
Texas became the world's leading executioner

11
00:00:56,001 --> 00:01:03,760
we certainly there's no secret that we have the most active death row most active death chamber

12
00:01:03,761 --> 00:01:10,400
in the country lethal injection is rapidly becoming the preferred method of execution

13
00:01:10,401 --> 00:01:16,240
throughout the united states critics believe that it is far from painless and that it can

14
00:01:16,241 --> 00:01:23,700
take a long time to complete the procedure larry fitzgerald disagrees i can tell you how long

15
00:01:23,701 --> 00:01:25,940
it takes for the chemicals to work.

16
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:29,060
We executed an inmate by the name of Jonathan Nobles,

17
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:32,420
and he apologized and recited scriptures as his final statement.

18
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:37,280
He told the warden the signal to start the process

19
00:01:37,281 --> 00:01:39,740
would be when he started singing,

20
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:43,300
and he sang Silent Night.

21
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:48,040
And when he got to mother and child, he was dead.

22
00:01:53,700 --> 00:02:00,620
After centuries of brutal executions, the quest for the most humane method of ending

23
00:02:00,621 --> 00:02:05,460
human life continues.

24
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:11,620
It's been a long, dark journey.

25
00:02:11,621 --> 00:02:17,540
Thousands of years ago, as early civilizations developed, conflicts threatened the survival

26
00:02:17,541 --> 00:02:21,260
of communities.

27
00:02:21,261 --> 00:02:25,140
Chieftains and leaders were called upon to hear grievances and make judgments.

28
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,680
Gradually, codes of conduct were drawn up,

29
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:33,040
and along with these early laws came the concept of punishment.

30
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:38,580
In almost all societies, the punishment for the worst crimes was death.

31
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:46,660
The first tools of execution probably included fists, sticks, and stones.

32
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:50,900
Well, with stoning, it's almost instantaneous.

33
00:02:51,260 --> 00:03:01,600
If the community decides that someone is guilty of something, the community usually will take it upon themselves almost immediately, almost in the form of a lynching.

34
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:11,680
Although stoning was used on both sexes, it was predominantly used on women and people convicted of heresy, sorcery and witchcraft.

35
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:18,960
Some early cultures believed that criminal behavior was a manifestation of demonic possession.

36
00:03:18,961 --> 00:03:24,200
that the evil spirits could be transmitted through personal contact.

37
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:29,760
Stoning became an effective method of killing at a distance,

38
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:33,360
where a mob would continue to hurl stones at the condemned,

39
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:36,040
eventually causing death by trauma.

40
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:44,300
In the Bronze Age, the development of metal began a new chapter in death devices.

41
00:03:44,301 --> 00:03:53,620
In military executions, the sword was used to decapitate people found guilty of treason,

42
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:56,340
cowardice, or mutiny.

43
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:04,840
Ancient Rome introduced the practice of execution as public entertainment, where condemned men

44
00:04:04,841 --> 00:04:10,180
and women were sentenced to combat animals, or gladiators, before crowds of spectators.

45
00:04:10,181 --> 00:04:19,740
the Romans also introduced crucifixion and the idea of death compounded by prolonged physical

46
00:04:19,741 --> 00:04:28,160
suffering victims of the cross could take days or even weeks to die crucifixion a soldier would

47
00:04:28,161 --> 00:04:35,900
strap them up and the community would come by and be able to stone them stab them break their bones

48
00:04:35,901 --> 00:04:38,720
and generally would take quite some time to die.

49
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:42,520
Dehydration or muscle collapse would suffocate them.

50
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:47,320
Some 300 years after the death of Christ,

51
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:50,800
the Roman Emperor Constantine abolished crucifixion

52
00:04:50,801 --> 00:04:52,840
after his conversion to Christianity.

53
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:57,260
Throughout the Middle Ages,

54
00:04:57,440 --> 00:05:00,280
the axe became the blade of the medieval criminal courts,

55
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:03,880
and its widespread use created a new position in the state,

56
00:05:03,881 --> 00:05:08,100
that of the headsman, the first executioner.

57
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:13,480
But execution by blade was a crude and messy business.

58
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:16,160
There was still a lot that could go wrong.

59
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:21,380
In medieval times, it was not uncommon for a person being executed

60
00:05:21,381 --> 00:05:24,560
to have to bribe the headsman to make sure that his aim was true

61
00:05:24,561 --> 00:05:25,880
and his blade was sharp.

62
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:30,060
God help you if you had a headsman who didn't like you

63
00:05:30,061 --> 00:05:32,900
because he might take more than one stroke to do the job.

64
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:40,340
In the 16th century, during the Holy Inquisition, numerous death and torture devices were developed.

65
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:44,340
Two survived to become official execution methods in Europe.

66
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:48,320
Both were slow, horrible ways to die.

67
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:53,980
Burning at the stake was made famous by the execution of Joan of Arc.

68
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:02,280
Breaking on the wheel became widely used all over Europe, especially in Germany and France.

69
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:10,140
Breaking on the wheel involved binding the nude victim, man or woman, flat or upright on a giant wooden wheel.

70
00:06:11,060 --> 00:06:20,020
As the wheel was rotated, several executioners wielding heavy metal clubs and hammers smashed the victim's limbs, joints, and skull.

71
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:27,100
This brutal method would eventually be replaced by a new mechanical device, which was supposed to be quick and painless.

72
00:06:27,101 --> 00:06:32,380
Next, the strange story of the guillotine

73
00:06:32,381 --> 00:06:38,820
Stoning is still practiced in six countries

74
00:06:38,821 --> 00:06:44,720
Pakistan, Iran, Afghanistan, the Grenadines, Sudan and the United Arab Emirates

75
00:06:44,721 --> 00:06:48,820
Death Devices will return on Modern Marvels

76
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:56,940
On a hot August day in 1788

77
00:06:57,660 --> 00:07:00,560
France's high executioner, Charles-Henri Saint-Saëns,

78
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:05,060
ascended the scaffold to carry out the execution of a man by breaking on the wheel.

79
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:16,560
The assembled mob of onlookers suddenly rushed the scaffold and freed the condemned man.

80
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:20,780
The scaffold and wheel were smashed and set on fire.

81
00:07:20,781 --> 00:07:25,660
When news of the riot reached the palace

82
00:07:25,661 --> 00:07:29,320
King Louis XVI, now sensing the impending revolt

83
00:07:29,321 --> 00:07:31,960
granted the prisoner a full pardon

84
00:07:31,961 --> 00:07:34,820
and abolished the wheel throughout France

85
00:07:34,821 --> 00:07:40,020
Three years later, after the fall of the Bastille

86
00:07:40,021 --> 00:07:43,620
the National Assembly sought a new execution method

87
00:07:43,621 --> 00:07:46,620
to be used on all people sentenced to death

88
00:07:46,621 --> 00:07:48,700
regardless of rank or class

89
00:07:48,701 --> 00:08:02,701
Ever since the time of the French Revolution, people have decided that capital punishment should be the end of life as opposed to infliction of pain.

90
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:09,220
A committee was formed.

91
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:11,348
At its head was Dr.

92
00:08:11,349 --> 00:08:16,080
Antoine Louis, physician to the king and secretary to the Academy of Surgery.

93
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:20,518
Also on the committee was Dr.

94
00:08:20,519 --> 00:08:25,720
Joseph Inez Guillotin, a professor of anatomy at the Facility of Medicine in Paris.

95
00:08:27,460 --> 00:08:33,000
Guillotin and the others had been influenced by earlier beheading machines, such as the Scottish Maiden,

96
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:41,280
the Italian Mania, and the Halifax Jebet.

97
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:44,540
It was a very crude guillotine of the time.

98
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:48,340
The blade, it was not sharp, it was more just a big metal chunk.

99
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:51,640
and it would knock the head off or crush the neck.

100
00:08:53,660 --> 00:08:55,500
Sketches and plans were submitted.

101
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:58,300
Two major improvements were the result.

102
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:01,360
First was a convex or crescent-shaped blade.

103
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,220
The second was the lunette, Little Moon,

104
00:09:05,221 --> 00:09:08,960
a hinged two-part yoke which immobilized the victim's neck.

105
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:18,200
The guillotine was invented with the notion that the blade would quickly sever the head,

106
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:24,740
with absolute assurance, and it was believed at the time that that would end life instantly.

107
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:32,480
The crescent shape was soon replaced by an angular blade.

108
00:09:34,580 --> 00:09:42,200
According to one historian's account, this sloping blade design was the idea of the king himself, Louis XVI.

109
00:09:42,201 --> 00:09:52,560
Upon seeing the sketches, the king, an amateur locksmith, well known for his mechanical aptitude, insisted that the shape of the convex blade was all wrong.

110
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:57,560
Grabbing a pen, the king sketched in a triangular blade with a beveled edge.

111
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:04,540
He contended that this blade would cut much cleaner and could accommodate virtually any size neck.

112
00:10:05,300 --> 00:10:07,800
These elements were incorporated into the final design.

113
00:10:07,801 --> 00:10:13,460
The new machine was tested on cadavers and cattle

114
00:10:13,461 --> 00:10:16,780
and finally deemed ready to be put into service

115
00:10:16,781 --> 00:10:21,300
On April 25th of 1792

116
00:10:21,301 --> 00:10:25,540
the first man to die under its blade was a thief and assassin

117
00:10:25,541 --> 00:10:27,540
named Jacques Nicolas Pelletier

118
00:10:27,541 --> 00:10:33,680
The crowds marveled at the machine's deadly speed and precision

119
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:40,100
Soon, a steady stream of victims would be hauled up the scaffold to meet the new modern machine.

120
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:54,300
Nine months after the first guillotine execution, the king and his wife, Marie Antoinette, would experience firsthand the effectiveness of the sloping blade design.

121
00:10:54,301 --> 00:11:02,660
Ironically, the name of the man who had contributed the least to the design of the machine

122
00:11:02,661 --> 00:11:05,560
was the name it would carry throughout history.

123
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:10,980
But it would be long after the French Revolution

124
00:11:10,981 --> 00:11:13,520
that the guillotine's bloodiest chapter would be written.

125
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:21,080
In 1933, Hitler celebrated his rise to power

126
00:11:21,081 --> 00:11:23,960
with the construction and testing of his first guillotine.

127
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:32,760
So impressed was he by its function that he ordered 20 more constructed and pressed into immediate service.

128
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:38,580
In addition to the millions murdered by gas and machine guns,

129
00:11:39,380 --> 00:11:45,480
Nazi records seized after the war testify that between 1933 and 1945,

130
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:50,880
16,500 people were murdered by Hitler's headsmen.

131
00:11:50,881 --> 00:11:55,680
whereas execution by guillotine during the French Revolution

132
00:11:55,681 --> 00:11:58,560
totaled only 2,600.

133
00:12:01,300 --> 00:12:05,120
The last public guillotining in France occurred in 1939

134
00:12:05,121 --> 00:12:09,120
with the execution of serial killer Eugène Wiedemann.

135
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:13,360
The event was secretly recorded on film.

136
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:16,340
When images from the film were published,

137
00:12:16,341 --> 00:12:19,680
the guillotine was moved behind prison walls.

138
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:28,400
The last use of the French guillotine was in 1977.

139
00:12:30,140 --> 00:12:34,500
In the early 1980s, all capital punishment was abolished in France.

140
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:42,260
Across the English Channel, a different method of execution had come into its own.

141
00:12:42,880 --> 00:12:47,520
It required only a tree and a rope. Gravity did the rest.

142
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:56,640
Although hanging is believed to have originated in Persia nearly 2,500 years ago, it was developed

143
00:12:56,641 --> 00:12:58,960
into a craft by English executioners.

144
00:13:00,940 --> 00:13:04,840
In 1500, there were eight hanging offenses in English law.

145
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:12,260
By the end of the 19th century, various acts of parliament had raised the number to 225.

146
00:13:12,840 --> 00:13:16,900
The prisons overflowed as more criminals were sentenced to die.

147
00:13:16,901 --> 00:13:24,800
London's original Tyburn gallows, just outside the city, was a crude single-beam structure.

148
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:32,620
Prisoners were either hauled up manually or left swinging when a ladder or cart was pulled out from under them.

149
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:42,760
As the number of executions increased in the late 1500s, the single-beam was replaced by a three-beam structure,

150
00:13:42,761 --> 00:13:46,880
which would gain infamy as the Tyburn Triple Tree.

151
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:52,780
The Triple Tree could accommodate as many as 24 prisoners at one time.

152
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:56,260
These public hangings attracted huge crowds.

153
00:13:57,500 --> 00:14:00,260
While some Londoners treated these executions as festivals,

154
00:14:01,440 --> 00:14:04,180
still others were repulsed and sickened.

155
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:07,800
In London, when they would hang people at Tyburn,

156
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:11,560
it was not unusual for there to be botched execution

157
00:14:11,561 --> 00:14:14,160
where the individual was dropped off the back of a cart

158
00:14:14,161 --> 00:14:17,200
and the knot was not in a proper spot,

159
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:19,860
and these individuals dangled and suffocated.

160
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:25,680
Public opinion began to grow against these gruesome spectacles.

161
00:14:26,540 --> 00:14:30,020
The government was called on to find a more efficient method.

162
00:14:31,020 --> 00:14:32,180
Change came slowly.

163
00:14:33,580 --> 00:14:35,200
Finally, in 1759,

164
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:39,360
the triple tree was replaced by the first trap door structure.

165
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:43,860
This ushered in what was known as the New Drop Era.

166
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:47,820
The British military, which really studied it,

167
00:14:48,220 --> 00:14:51,300
looked into the precise weight of the body,

168
00:14:51,820 --> 00:14:56,540
which would help calculate the precise drop of the individual.

169
00:14:58,260 --> 00:15:01,500
William Calcraft, the longest-serving English hangman,

170
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:04,520
is credited with the development of the short drop method.

171
00:15:04,521 --> 00:15:11,200
When the trap opened, the prisoner fell 8 to 12 inches.

172
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:18,620
Though an improvement over Tyburn, slow strangulation was still the usual result.

173
00:15:20,580 --> 00:15:24,880
Soon after, a new hanging technique was introduced, the long drop method.

174
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:30,420
The long drop involved a fall of 6 to 10 feet, sometimes with the addition of weights.

175
00:15:30,421 --> 00:15:37,660
A critical knot placement was also determined along the angle of the left jaw, just behind the ear.

176
00:15:40,260 --> 00:15:44,560
This method snapped the neck, causing instant unconsciousness.

177
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:47,440
Death by asphyxiation soon followed.

178
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:52,620
The long drop was not without its problems.

179
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:58,300
Too long a drop or too much weight could result in the decapitation of the condemned.

180
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:04,880
Although the English practice of hanging had exported well to America,

181
00:16:05,780 --> 00:16:09,460
England's master executioners and their techniques had not.

182
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:17,580
In early America, with its far-flung seats of law, crude lynchings by local sheriffs became prevalent.

183
00:16:19,340 --> 00:16:22,940
It was unsightly and it was uncivilized, barbaric.

184
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:26,960
So there was an attempt at the same time to do away with the lynchings

185
00:16:26,961 --> 00:16:31,280
and then to create a more civilized environment for executions.

186
00:16:31,640 --> 00:16:34,860
As public outcries against hanging grew louder,

187
00:16:35,440 --> 00:16:38,040
many cities began to look for an alternative.

188
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:42,460
The story of the machine that would replace the gallows

189
00:16:42,461 --> 00:16:45,780
is one of the strangest tales in the history of American invention.

190
00:16:46,460 --> 00:16:50,100
Next, the story of old Sparky, the electric chair.

191
00:16:50,101 --> 00:16:58,680
Hanging remains the second most common execution method in the world.

192
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:04,380
In 1999, 139 people were hanged in 11 countries.

193
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:08,520
Death devices will return on Modern Marvels.

194
00:17:10,420 --> 00:17:12,860
It is the last of a vanishing breed.

195
00:17:14,080 --> 00:17:16,860
Housed deep inside Holman Prison in Atmore, Alabama,

196
00:17:17,620 --> 00:17:21,260
It is one of the last electric chairs still available for use in America.

197
00:17:22,380 --> 00:17:24,380
The one in Alabama was called the Big Yellow Mama.

198
00:17:24,860 --> 00:17:27,560
Because the electric chair was associated with a criminal element,

199
00:17:27,880 --> 00:17:31,560
there was a lot of nicknames that came for it.

200
00:17:31,660 --> 00:17:33,720
The one in Florida was called Old Sparky.

201
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:37,620
The one at Sing Sing was known as Old Sparky as well.

202
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:43,060
There has always been a mystique surrounding the chair,

203
00:17:43,061 --> 00:17:45,860
and many of America's most notorious criminals

204
00:17:45,861 --> 00:17:47,700
have had the questionable distinction

205
00:17:47,701 --> 00:17:49,520
of dying within its framework.

206
00:17:51,660 --> 00:17:53,180
From Ted Bundy

207
00:17:53,181 --> 00:17:56,080
to Sacco and Vanzetti

208
00:17:56,081 --> 00:17:59,000
to Bruno Hauptmann,

209
00:17:59,460 --> 00:18:01,180
the convicted Lindbergh baby killer,

210
00:18:01,580 --> 00:18:03,600
the chair has claimed hundreds of victims.

211
00:18:04,700 --> 00:18:08,360
The story of the chair dates back to the late 1800s

212
00:18:08,361 --> 00:18:11,900
when a new form of power was changing the way Americans lived.

213
00:18:11,901 --> 00:18:16,660
electricity. It would soon change the way condemned criminals died.

214
00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:22,420
By 1885, several human rights groups were calling for an end to hanging.

215
00:18:23,380 --> 00:18:26,463
In 1888, the new governor of New York, David B.

216
00:18:26,464 --> 00:18:29,940
Hill, had joined the movement to abolish the gallows.

217
00:18:31,380 --> 00:18:34,620
After witnessing a particularly gruesome execution of a woman,

218
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:41,620
he instructed his legislature to begin a search for a new, more humane means of putting people to death.

219
00:18:41,900 --> 00:18:44,260
A committee was formed.

220
00:18:44,720 --> 00:18:49,060
One of its members, Dr. A.P. Southwick, a prominent dentist,

221
00:18:49,500 --> 00:18:53,480
had witnessed the accidental electrocution of a man who had touched a terminal

222
00:18:53,481 --> 00:18:56,420
on an unshielded electrical generator.

223
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:01,620
The Death Commission, as it was called in the papers,

224
00:19:02,160 --> 00:19:06,180
analyzed over 40 procedures and narrowed these down to four.

225
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:08,760
Garrett, a method of strangulation.

226
00:19:10,040 --> 00:19:10,740
Guillotine.

227
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:13,200
injection,

228
00:19:14,540 --> 00:19:16,700
and electrocution.

229
00:19:17,640 --> 00:19:21,800
The committee sent out a questionnaire to judges, doctors, and scientists,

230
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:24,820
asking them to endorse one of these four methods.

231
00:19:25,620 --> 00:19:29,300
One of the respondents who favored electrocution was Thomas Edison.

232
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:31,960
He was a major personality at the time.

233
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:34,960
Everybody knew him, and the other members of that committee

234
00:19:34,961 --> 00:19:37,640
simply went along with his recommendations.

235
00:19:37,641 --> 00:19:43,800
While the members of the commission had been battling over a new method of execution,

236
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:48,200
Edison had been fighting a battle of his own and losing.

237
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:52,400
The battle was called the War of the Currents.

238
00:19:53,320 --> 00:19:58,540
Edison had invested his fortune and his reputation in his direct current utility system.

239
00:19:59,320 --> 00:20:01,740
He hoped to sell his system to the world.

240
00:20:02,240 --> 00:20:06,460
Thomas Edison was someone who favored direct current.

241
00:20:07,320 --> 00:20:09,960
George Westinghouse favored alternating current.

242
00:20:10,320 --> 00:20:13,680
In those days, it was unclear which really was going to win out.

243
00:20:15,720 --> 00:20:18,460
Alternating current was cheaper to produce and distribute.

244
00:20:19,480 --> 00:20:23,180
But with its higher voltages, it was considered by some to be more dangerous.

245
00:20:24,920 --> 00:20:31,120
Edison and his people took every opportunity to try to prove that AC was too dangerous for public use.

246
00:20:31,121 --> 00:20:43,535
When Southwick and the Death Commission came along with their electrocution idea, Edison saw an opportunity to sabotage Westinghouse and AC by recommending alternating

247
00:20:43,536 --> 00:20:46,880
current be used for the new execution device.

248
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:56,020
Harold Brown, a close Edison confidant, demonstrated to the committee how AC current could instantly kill animals.

249
00:20:56,021 --> 00:21:01,020
the commission was suitably impressed

250
00:21:01,021 --> 00:21:11,660
in 1888 when hanging was replaced by electrocution

251
00:21:11,661 --> 00:21:16,140
Brown was awarded the contract to build the first electrocution machine

252
00:21:16,141 --> 00:21:19,880
he worked closely with a dentist

253
00:21:19,881 --> 00:21:24,260
Dr. Southwick who would later be called the father of electrocution

254
00:21:24,261 --> 00:21:28,400
Alfred Southworth was a dentist from Buffalo

255
00:21:28,401 --> 00:21:30,600
and that's the reason that it became a chair

256
00:21:30,601 --> 00:21:32,300
I think it would have became like a table

257
00:21:32,301 --> 00:21:34,420
or a cabinet or something like that

258
00:21:34,421 --> 00:21:36,160
but it was a chair because he was a dentist

259
00:21:36,161 --> 00:21:38,260
and he was used to working with chairs

260
00:21:38,261 --> 00:21:41,240
After several trial prototypes

261
00:21:41,241 --> 00:21:44,120
Brown's final design was assembled by convict labor

262
00:21:44,121 --> 00:21:45,100
at Auburn Prison

263
00:21:45,101 --> 00:21:49,620
The chair is a simple wooden fixture

264
00:21:49,621 --> 00:21:51,360
that holds the prisoner in place

265
00:21:51,361 --> 00:21:55,420
Once the power is turned on

266
00:21:55,421 --> 00:22:00,320
Wet sponges conduct the current from the electrodes into the prisoner's body

267
00:22:00,321 --> 00:22:04,920
The brain shorts out and the heart stops

268
00:22:04,921 --> 00:22:08,540
Westinghouse didn't want to use his generator

269
00:22:08,541 --> 00:22:11,660
So he actually refused to sell New York State a generator

270
00:22:11,661 --> 00:22:15,760
So New York State then had to buy a Westinghouse generator

271
00:22:15,761 --> 00:22:19,140
From Rio de Janeiro in Brazil and bring it to Auburn Prison

272
00:22:19,900 --> 00:22:25,500
On August 6, 1890, an alcoholic peddler named William Kemmler,

273
00:22:25,920 --> 00:22:30,620
who was convicted of killing his girlfriend, was the first person to die in the electric chair.

274
00:22:31,980 --> 00:22:36,740
After the first jolt of 17 seconds, Kemmler was still breathing.

275
00:22:37,600 --> 00:22:40,580
A second jolt of 30 seconds finally did the job.

276
00:22:41,340 --> 00:22:46,160
When it was over, the chamber was filled with the smell of burnt flesh.

277
00:22:46,161 --> 00:22:49,080
Kemmler's head was smoking

278
00:22:49,081 --> 00:22:52,220
he never moved or breathed again

279
00:22:52,221 --> 00:22:56,580
the triumph of modern civilization had come at last

280
00:22:56,581 --> 00:23:02,560
newspapers around the world condemned the execution

281
00:23:02,561 --> 00:23:04,980
George Westinghouse remarked

282
00:23:04,981 --> 00:23:08,080
they could have done a better job with an axe

283
00:23:08,081 --> 00:23:12,160
nevertheless the chair was here to stay

284
00:23:12,161 --> 00:23:16,080
even though the technology was improved with higher voltages

285
00:23:16,081 --> 00:23:20,460
and better connections, the botches and problems continued.

286
00:23:22,040 --> 00:23:28,480
By 1906, three other states, Ohio, Massachusetts, and New Jersey,

287
00:23:29,040 --> 00:23:32,600
had abolished hanging and replaced it with electrocution.

288
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:39,800
Executions in the chair peaked between the 1920s and 1930s

289
00:23:39,801 --> 00:23:45,040
when a man named Robert Green Elliott was the chief executioner in New York.

290
00:23:46,080 --> 00:23:51,640
One of the controversial executions he performed was that of Ruth Snyder.

291
00:23:53,260 --> 00:23:57,660
She had been convicted of the murder of her husband in 1927.

292
00:23:58,840 --> 00:24:04,480
The circumstances around Snyder's execution may have influenced public opinion about the chair.

293
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:12,900
There was a lot of interest in the United States in the 1920s about how the electric chair actually worked

294
00:24:12,901 --> 00:24:15,360
because no one had actually seen it in operation.

295
00:24:15,361 --> 00:24:19,720
The idea of actually photographing somebody was a journalistic coup.

296
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:23,720
The New York Daily News succeeded in getting that picture.

297
00:24:24,520 --> 00:24:29,560
The photographer, who was one of the witnesses, attached a hidden camera to his ankle

298
00:24:29,561 --> 00:24:32,660
and ran a cable release up through his pants.

299
00:24:33,640 --> 00:24:37,160
He snapped this picture at the moment the execution began.

300
00:24:37,161 --> 00:24:48,740
In 1946, a traveling electric chair was used to execute Willie Francis in Louisiana.

301
00:24:49,660 --> 00:24:53,160
When the switch was thrown, the chair malfunctioned.

302
00:24:53,700 --> 00:24:55,040
The generator exploded.

303
00:24:57,180 --> 00:25:02,200
Willie was returned to his cell stunned and complaining of a taste of cold peanut butter.

304
00:25:04,220 --> 00:25:07,040
Appeals to spare Francis were filed but denied.

305
00:25:07,160 --> 00:25:10,640
A year later, the chair functioned as designed.

306
00:25:11,360 --> 00:25:15,760
Francis became the first and only man to be electrocuted twice.

307
00:25:18,620 --> 00:25:24,660
In 1953, convicted spies Julius and Ethel Rosenberg were sentenced to the chair.

308
00:25:26,940 --> 00:25:33,800
The Rosenberg's executions proved once again the unpredictable nature of killing people with electricity.

309
00:25:33,801 --> 00:25:39,760
It only took two jolts of electricity to kill Julius,

310
00:25:40,060 --> 00:25:44,360
but when it came to Ethel, they had to use five jolts of electricity to kill her,

311
00:25:44,480 --> 00:25:48,220
even though it was the same chair, the same electricity, everything else was the same.

312
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:55,720
At the peak of its popularity, 25 states used the chair as their main method of execution.

313
00:25:57,480 --> 00:26:01,240
By 1989, only 14 states still used the chair.

314
00:26:03,800 --> 00:26:12,800
Today, though infrequent, the use of the electric chair did survive into the 21st century.

315
00:26:12,801 --> 00:26:19,800
Alabama carried out the first electrocution of the new millennium on January 7, 2000.

316
00:26:19,801 --> 00:26:30,800
Next, weapons of war become tools of execution.

317
00:26:30,801 --> 00:26:36,760
Lureta's old Sparky was constructed from lumber taken from the gallows it replaced.

318
00:26:36,761 --> 00:26:46,040
Death Devices will return on Modern Marvels.

319
00:26:46,041 --> 00:26:52,240
The firing squad is the most commonly used method of execution throughout the world.

320
00:26:52,241 --> 00:27:00,760
In the United States, three states currently have provisions for firing squad executions.

321
00:27:00,761 --> 00:27:07,240
The most recent was in 1996 in Utah, when a condemned man named John Taylor chose firing

322
00:27:07,241 --> 00:27:09,040
squad over lethal injection.

323
00:27:09,041 --> 00:27:15,600
I'd just like to say for my family, for my friends, as the poem was written, remember

324
00:27:15,601 --> 00:27:16,660
me, but let me go.

325
00:27:17,500 --> 00:27:22,020
There was a man being hanged in Delaware, a woman who was scheduled to die by execution

326
00:27:22,021 --> 00:27:27,580
and to be executed in Illinois, and the firing squad in Utah, and all of the focus was on

327
00:27:27,581 --> 00:27:28,281
Utah.

328
00:27:28,282 --> 00:27:35,260
For Taylor's execution, Utah designed a special chair made with its own bulletproof backstop.

329
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:39,660
On the day of the execution, Taylor was strapped into the chair.

330
00:27:40,600 --> 00:27:43,120
Restraints held his hands, feet, and neck.

331
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:45,920
A black hood was placed over his head.

332
00:27:46,640 --> 00:27:51,640
At 12.03 a.m., 20 feet from the firing partition,

333
00:27:52,480 --> 00:27:56,040
Five marksmen, armed with .30-30 caliber rifles,

334
00:27:56,640 --> 00:27:59,580
fired at a white cloth target pinned over his heart.

335
00:28:01,340 --> 00:28:04,740
Four minutes later, a doctor pronounced Taylor dead.

336
00:28:06,380 --> 00:28:09,900
Taylor was not the first person to die by firing squad in Utah.

337
00:28:11,080 --> 00:28:16,760
In 1977, a 10-year moratorium on capital punishment in the United States ended.

338
00:28:16,761 --> 00:28:23,340
The first execution of the new era took place in Utah

339
00:28:23,341 --> 00:28:27,500
The condemned man's name was Gary Gilmore

340
00:28:27,501 --> 00:28:33,560
That no factual issues have been presented to the board

341
00:28:33,561 --> 00:28:37,040
Which shows any reason for the board to disagree

342
00:28:37,041 --> 00:28:40,920
With the sentence as imposed by the district court

343
00:28:40,921 --> 00:28:45,020
He was given a choice between hanging and the firing squad

344
00:28:45,640 --> 00:28:47,140
He chose firing squad.

345
00:28:49,580 --> 00:28:52,600
A simple office chair was used for the execution.

346
00:28:56,520 --> 00:29:02,960
After the Gilmore execution, the media was all there sticking their fingers in the holes and taking pictures.

347
00:29:02,961 --> 00:29:16,961
The exact origins of the firing squad protocol are unknown.

348
00:29:18,960 --> 00:29:21,620
But as the rifle replaced the sword on the battlefield,

349
00:29:22,380 --> 00:29:25,240
it also replaced it in military executions.

350
00:29:25,241 --> 00:29:33,700
In 1866, a Utah sheriff named John Gillespie oversaw an execution.

351
00:29:35,400 --> 00:29:39,420
A firing squad of five men was assembled and concealed inside a tent.

352
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:49,260
Once the seated criminal was blindfolded, the sheriff opened the flaps of the tent and gave the order to fire.

353
00:29:49,261 --> 00:29:55,660
Traditionally, one rifle in the squad is loaded with a blank round

354
00:29:55,661 --> 00:30:00,400
This prevents the members from knowing who fired the fatal shots

355
00:30:00,401 --> 00:30:04,400
But like the headsman 100 years before him

356
00:30:04,401 --> 00:30:07,920
The marksman proved to be the weak link in the process

357
00:30:07,921 --> 00:30:11,860
The accusation has been made several times

358
00:30:11,861 --> 00:30:14,780
The firing squad either intentionally misses or intentionally maims

359
00:30:15,320 --> 00:30:19,520
Eliseo Murray's 1952 was one of those, where at the distance of about 15 feet,

360
00:30:19,580 --> 00:30:20,740
all of the firing squad missed.

361
00:30:20,860 --> 00:30:23,740
He was hit in the hip and in the shoulder and the arm.

362
00:30:24,140 --> 00:30:27,300
And so there's some debate going on about whether to reload or not,

363
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:29,340
and he bled to death in the process.

364
00:30:32,180 --> 00:30:37,680
In 1912, the state of Nevada attempted to remove the human element from the procedure

365
00:30:37,681 --> 00:30:41,100
by designing and building a firing squad machine.

366
00:30:41,101 --> 00:30:45,720
The enclosed structure came with its own steel backdrop.

367
00:30:46,720 --> 00:30:48,920
It was fitted with three pre-aimed rifles.

368
00:30:50,080 --> 00:30:53,840
The trigger mechanisms were controlled by three lengths of taut cord.

369
00:30:54,920 --> 00:30:57,160
Only one cord actually fired the machine.

370
00:30:58,420 --> 00:31:01,060
When the cords were cut, the rifles discharged.

371
00:31:02,940 --> 00:31:07,220
In the execution of Andriza Mirkovic in May of 1912,

372
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:09,660
the machine functioned perfectly.

373
00:31:10,500 --> 00:31:15,060
However, firing squad executions were coming under increasing public attack.

374
00:31:17,060 --> 00:31:21,160
Nine years later, Nevada developed a completely new technology

375
00:31:21,161 --> 00:31:24,280
that was supposed to be quick and painless,

376
00:31:24,640 --> 00:31:27,000
with no outwardly visible side effects.

377
00:31:27,780 --> 00:31:30,900
This new method was called lethal gas.

378
00:31:31,840 --> 00:31:35,600
The governor at the time, in 1921,

379
00:31:35,720 --> 00:31:38,480
was actually an opponent of capital punishment, Governor Boyle.

380
00:31:38,481 --> 00:31:51,240
And by supporting the development of a humane execution bill, he believed that he was helping to ensure that capital punishment would not be carried out in the state.

381
00:31:52,100 --> 00:31:56,495
The next prisoner to be sentenced to die in Nevada was a man named G.

382
00:31:56,496 --> 00:31:58,660
John, who was convicted of murder.

383
00:31:58,661 --> 00:31:59,614
It was 1924.

384
00:31:59,615 --> 00:32:08,760
There was a new governor, and there was tremendous pressure to carry out the execution with the new gas technology.

385
00:32:11,020 --> 00:32:17,520
Prison authorities hurriedly tried to prepare a means to carry out the execution.

386
00:32:18,660 --> 00:32:27,720
They found a barbershop, a stone barbershop within the prison that seemed to be appropriate for the use of a gas chamber.

387
00:32:28,660 --> 00:32:32,660
They sealed off one room to create an airtight chamber.

388
00:32:32,661 --> 00:32:38,560
From the adjoining room, liquid cyanide could be pumped through the wall by the use of a tire pump.

389
00:32:38,561 --> 00:32:44,560
Upon hitting the warm air, the cyanide transformed into the lethal gas.

390
00:32:44,561 --> 00:32:48,560
They tested the system successfully on two cats.

391
00:32:48,561 --> 00:32:50,893
On the morning of the execution, G.

392
00:32:50,894 --> 00:32:55,560
John was secured inside the makeshift chamber and strapped to a chair.

393
00:32:55,561 --> 00:32:56,430
to a chair.

394
00:32:56,431 --> 00:33:02,760
It was a cold day, and they needed a temperature of 75 degrees for the procedure

395
00:33:02,761 --> 00:33:03,461
to work.

396
00:33:03,462 --> 00:33:09,220
They placed an electric heater inside the chamber to warm up the cyanide, so that

397
00:33:09,221 --> 00:33:12,072
it would vaporize into a gas.

398
00:33:12,073 --> 00:33:17,480
Witnesses and officials watched through sealed windows.

399
00:33:18,480 --> 00:33:24,660
Though the execution took longer than expected, John finally collapsed and was declared dead.

400
00:33:25,560 --> 00:33:32,560
In 1929, Nevada built a new gas chamber using convict labor.

401
00:33:32,561 --> 00:33:40,560
At its peak, 11 states used lethal gas as their primary execution method.

402
00:33:40,561 --> 00:33:45,560
At the same time as the gas chamber was gaining acceptance in the United States,

403
00:33:45,561 --> 00:33:53,560
Nazi Germany was also experimenting with lethal gas

404
00:33:53,561 --> 00:34:00,560
gas as a way to perform mass murder. It is believed that following World War II, the

405
00:34:02,240 --> 00:34:07,720
stigma of the Nazi gas chambers kept lethal gas from ever being widely accepted as a death

406
00:34:07,721 --> 00:34:09,947
device in the United States.

407
00:34:09,948 --> 00:34:14,720
Lethal gas remains the most dangerous, expensive and complex

408
00:34:14,721 --> 00:34:19,184
the most dangerous, expensive and complex method of execution.

409
00:34:19,185 --> 00:34:20,840
Today it is rarely used

410
00:34:20,841 --> 00:34:24,480
in the U.S., though in a few states it remains an option.

411
00:34:27,860 --> 00:34:36,284
In 1999, Walter Legrand, an Arizona man sentenced to death, chose that option.

412
00:34:36,285 --> 00:34:37,580
The staff of

413
00:34:37,581 --> 00:34:42,960
Arizona State Prison went into action, preparing for this complex and potentially dangerous

414
00:34:42,961 --> 00:34:43,661
event.

415
00:34:43,662 --> 00:34:52,000
Because it is a very risky execution in terms of the risk to the people who are

416
00:34:52,001 --> 00:34:58,051
performing the execution, it has to be done very carefully.

417
00:34:58,052 --> 00:35:00,820
Sulfuric acid fills a small

418
00:35:00,821 --> 00:35:07,040
vat directly beneath the chair. A cyanide egg sits above the acid on a conical trap

419
00:35:07,041 --> 00:35:07,741
door.

420
00:35:07,742 --> 00:35:12,940
The final lever opens the trap, dropping the egg into the acid where it begins to vaporize.

421
00:35:12,960 --> 00:35:20,200
That's where they mix and then it comes up through underneath the chair and consumes and envelops the inmate.

422
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:26,340
Arizona State Prison maintains many safety precautions throughout the operation.

423
00:35:27,120 --> 00:35:31,240
You don't have a whole lot of time should an accident occur.

424
00:35:31,700 --> 00:35:41,600
You have approximately seven seconds for the antidote to be injected into the heart to avoid a fatality.

425
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:49,280
Right outside of the building, we have medical personnel standing by with syringes in the ready.

426
00:35:49,660 --> 00:35:54,840
They have the antidote in the syringe ready for injection should something occur.

427
00:35:55,600 --> 00:35:59,840
Once the inmate is pronounced dead, the team removes the body.

428
00:36:01,060 --> 00:36:03,640
They neutralize the cyanide gas with ammonia.

429
00:36:06,280 --> 00:36:10,360
It is then pumped into the atmosphere through a towering exhaust stack.

430
00:36:10,361 --> 00:36:17,240
Once the chamber has been thoroughly cleansed, the execution is complete.

431
00:36:18,600 --> 00:36:23,860
Next, intravenous executions and beyond.

432
00:36:27,820 --> 00:36:36,340
Between 1930 and 1980, 945 men and 7 women were put to death in America's gas chambers.

433
00:36:36,341 --> 00:36:41,340
Death Devices will return on Modern Marvels.

434
00:36:49,340 --> 00:36:55,160
A lethal injection facility exudes the calm clinical atmosphere of a hospital setting.

435
00:36:55,161 --> 00:37:04,520
Flanked by a special squad of officers called a tie-down team

436
00:37:04,521 --> 00:37:09,580
The inmate is brought into the injection chamber and strapped face up on the gurney

437
00:37:09,581 --> 00:37:13,140
His arms are spread out wide

438
00:37:13,141 --> 00:37:15,700
An IV is inserted in each arm

439
00:37:15,701 --> 00:37:19,120
Only one will be used for the actual injection

440
00:37:19,121 --> 00:37:21,060
The second serves as a backup

441
00:37:21,061 --> 00:37:28,060
The IV tubes run from the inmates arms through a port in the wall to the chemical room,

442
00:37:28,061 --> 00:37:34,060
where the executioners are concealed by a one-way mirror.

443
00:37:34,061 --> 00:37:43,060
The Huntsville unit in Texas uses trained paramedics who volunteer to perform the lethal injection procedure.

444
00:37:43,061 --> 00:37:53,640
During each execution, they inject three chemicals into the IV tubing by hand.

445
00:37:56,320 --> 00:38:00,800
First, five grams of sodium pentothal render the prisoner unconscious.

446
00:38:02,680 --> 00:38:11,620
Second, 100 milligrams of a curare-like drug, pancuronium bromide, paralyze the muscles and stop all lung function.

447
00:38:13,060 --> 00:38:19,340
Finally, 50 to 100 milligrams of potassium chloride shut down the heart.

448
00:38:21,840 --> 00:38:26,120
Although most American doctors refuse to participate in executions

449
00:38:26,121 --> 00:38:28,180
because it violates the Hippocratic Oath,

450
00:38:29,340 --> 00:38:32,920
doctors are sometimes needed to pronounce the inmate dead.

451
00:38:34,280 --> 00:38:36,580
Even though the cause of death is apparent,

452
00:38:37,080 --> 00:38:39,020
we would have to go through with an autopsy.

453
00:38:39,021 --> 00:38:42,820
But by having the physician do it, an autopsy is not required.

454
00:38:43,060 --> 00:38:53,280
The concept of using chemical poison to execute convicted criminals goes back to the days of Socrates, who was convicted of sedition.

455
00:38:54,680 --> 00:38:57,900
The senator said, you've got a choice.

456
00:38:58,340 --> 00:39:05,360
You can either leave Greece and admit you're wrong, or stay here and drink poison, drink hemlock.

457
00:39:05,900 --> 00:39:10,960
And he did have the dignity to administer his own poison and kill himself.

458
00:39:10,961 --> 00:39:17,780
But it was a form of execution because they had carried out a sentence, punished him and condemned him.

459
00:39:20,140 --> 00:39:27,240
During World War II, the Nazis performed lethal injections as part of their experiments with execution technology.

460
00:39:29,260 --> 00:39:36,380
Germany also was beginning a program euphemistically called a euthanasia program,

461
00:39:36,381 --> 00:39:42,420
which resulted in the deaths of many people.

462
00:39:43,760 --> 00:39:48,440
It wasn't until 1982 that the first lethal injection execution

463
00:39:48,441 --> 00:39:52,240
was performed as a method of capital punishment in the United States.

464
00:39:54,240 --> 00:39:58,040
Texas was the first in the nation to implement the procedure

465
00:39:58,041 --> 00:40:02,800
with the execution of Charlie Brooks, a convicted murderer.

466
00:40:02,801 --> 00:40:07,960
witnesses noted that Charlie Brooks wheezed

467
00:40:07,961 --> 00:40:10,500
and his stomach spasmed up and down

468
00:40:10,501 --> 00:40:11,900
as the drugs were administered

469
00:40:11,901 --> 00:40:15,120
the execution was applauded

470
00:40:15,121 --> 00:40:16,240
as a success

471
00:40:16,241 --> 00:40:21,640
in 1983 a lethal injection machine

472
00:40:21,641 --> 00:40:22,380
was developed

473
00:40:22,381 --> 00:40:25,880
two buttons were pushed simultaneously

474
00:40:25,881 --> 00:40:27,140
by two people

475
00:40:27,141 --> 00:40:29,220
to activate the flow of the drugs

476
00:40:29,221 --> 00:40:30,460
one at a time

477
00:40:30,461 --> 00:40:31,840
at an even pace

478
00:40:31,841 --> 00:40:40,400
only the computer would know which button had triggered the process

479
00:40:40,401 --> 00:40:44,080
this information was erased during every execution

480
00:40:44,081 --> 00:40:54,500
today most lethal injections are done manually to prevent back pressure from the vein from stopping

481
00:40:54,501 --> 00:40:59,933
the flow of the IV. A machine is not sensitive to back pressure.

482
00:40:59,934 --> 00:41:02,820
By having somebody who is trained,

483
00:41:03,380 --> 00:41:08,268
such as an EMT person, they can feel that pressure on the syringe.

484
00:41:08,269 --> 00:41:10,120
So that's why we do that.

485
00:41:11,000 --> 00:41:13,614
The system is still far from perfect.

486
00:41:13,615 --> 00:41:17,500
Problems can occur with the veins, such as having an IV

487
00:41:17,501 --> 00:41:21,700
fall out of the inmate's arm, sometimes spraying the chemicals all over the room.

488
00:41:22,580 --> 00:41:27,100
We've had, on two occasions, as I'm aware, what we call a blowout.

489
00:41:27,340 --> 00:41:31,420
That's where the IV is actually popped out of the arm.

490
00:41:33,220 --> 00:41:37,700
The most recent one occurred as we were just getting ready to do it,

491
00:41:37,780 --> 00:41:41,320
and the inmates looked at them and said, hey, it came out.

492
00:41:43,520 --> 00:41:48,860
For many years, the possibility of death row organ donation has been a topic of debate.

493
00:41:48,861 --> 00:41:52,960
But in most cases, it has proved impractical.

494
00:41:54,660 --> 00:41:58,620
One of the truly sad things about lethal injection

495
00:41:58,621 --> 00:42:02,940
is that the chemicals used in the execution are so pervasive,

496
00:42:03,300 --> 00:42:08,540
are so powerful, that it literally ruins all the organs, literally cooks them.

497
00:42:09,520 --> 00:42:14,680
One of the few execution methods that allows for organ donation is the firing squad.

498
00:42:16,420 --> 00:42:18,600
When Gary Gilmore was executed,

499
00:42:18,860 --> 00:42:20,800
He donated his organs.

500
00:42:22,640 --> 00:42:25,680
Gary Gilmore's eyes were transplanted.

501
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:28,600
There's a fellow living in Utah somewhere who has Gary Gilmore's eyes.

502
00:42:28,601 --> 00:42:33,000
There are certain parts of organs that can be used after firing squad executions.

503
00:42:36,100 --> 00:42:41,520
Writer Stuart Creek has proposed a new method he calls nitrogen asphyxiation,

504
00:42:41,860 --> 00:42:44,160
which he claims would not damage organs.

505
00:42:44,980 --> 00:42:49,840
When death penalty opponents began to sue,

506
00:42:50,640 --> 00:42:55,420
claiming that the death penalty was cruel and unusual punishment

507
00:42:55,421 --> 00:42:57,980
based on the way that it was administered,

508
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:01,040
gas chamber, electric chair, that type of thing,

509
00:43:01,880 --> 00:43:04,180
it occurred to me that nitrogen asphyxiation

510
00:43:04,181 --> 00:43:09,800
was a completely painless way of administering death.

511
00:43:09,801 --> 00:43:14,360
Nitrogen is odorless and tasteless.

512
00:43:14,361 --> 00:43:20,680
Since it is one of the components of our atmosphere, it is far less dangerous than poisonous gas.

513
00:43:20,681 --> 00:43:28,560
The victim would slip into unconsciousness, and death would follow caused by oxygen deprivation.

514
00:43:28,561 --> 00:43:33,560
As more and more countries abolish the death penalty,

515
00:43:33,561 --> 00:43:39,800
There is also the notion that all of these death devices could eventually become relics of the past.

516
00:43:42,060 --> 00:43:48,960
The United States is currently the only major Western nation that still employs the death penalty.

517
00:43:50,180 --> 00:43:56,300
38 states, the military, and the federal government all have capital punishment provisions.

518
00:43:57,080 --> 00:44:00,340
Each has its own laws about which method is used.

519
00:44:00,341 --> 00:44:06,040
It is the subject of much debate as the new millennium proceeds.

